1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:04,561
Welcome to AI Agents with
Python, Beginner to Production.

2
00:00:04,781 --> 00:00:10,293
Over this course you will go from
writing your first Python data structures to

3
00:00:10,293 --> 00:00:16,159
deploying a full AI agent application
with authentication, retrieval, tools, tests,

4
00:00:16,159 --> 00:00:17,219
and monitoring.

5
00:00:17,439 --> 00:00:22,879
This first lesson explains what an agent
actually is, how the course works, and then

6
00:00:22,879 --> 00:00:25,145
gets your Python environment ready.

7
00:00:26,445 --> 00:00:31,417
Let's start with a precise definition,
because the word agent is used loosely.

8
00:00:31,767 --> 00:00:36,360
An AI agent is a program that is
given a goal, not a fixed script.

9
00:00:36,580 --> 00:00:41,478
You describe what you want, for example,
research this topic and write me a brief.

10
00:00:41,828 --> 00:00:45,971
The program asks a language
model to decide what to do next.

11
00:00:46,191 --> 00:00:48,874
The model does not run anything by itself.

12
00:00:49,094 --> 00:00:53,936
It only returns a decision, as
text or as a structured tool request.

13
00:00:54,286 --> 00:00:59,809
Your Python code then performs that
action with a tool, such as a web search, a

14
00:00:59,809 --> 00:01:04,842
database query, or a calculator,
and feeds the result back to the model.

15
00:01:05,192 --> 00:01:06,644
This repeats in a loop.

16
00:01:06,864 --> 00:01:12,338
Decide, act, observe, and continue,
until the goal is reached or a safety limit

17
00:01:12,338 --> 00:01:12,962
stops it.

18
00:01:13,182 --> 00:01:17,983
That loop is the heart of every agent
in this course, and you will build it from

19
00:01:17,983 --> 00:01:19,183
scratch in Module 4.

20
00:01:20,483 --> 00:01:23,106
Here is how the
eighteen modules fit together.

21
00:01:23,456 --> 00:01:29,447
Part one builds your foundations: Python
for AI work, calling language model APIs,

22
00:01:29,447 --> 00:01:33,612
and prompt engineering with
validated, structured output.

23
00:01:33,962 --> 00:01:39,863
Part two builds agents from scratch, with
no framework hiding the details: the loop,

24
00:01:39,863 --> 00:01:43,305
tool calling, memory,
and making agents reliable.

25
00:01:43,655 --> 00:01:49,412
Part three connects agents to knowledge
and tools, with the Model Context Protocol,

26
00:01:49,412 --> 00:01:52,672
embeddings, and
retrieval-augmented generation.

27
00:01:53,022 --> 00:01:59,170
Part four covers orchestration:
LangGraph workflows, multi-agent systems, and web

28
00:01:59,170 --> 00:02:00,384
research agents.

29
00:02:00,734 --> 00:02:05,818
Part five is about quality and
security: testing and evaluating agents, and

30
00:02:05,818 --> 00:02:09,546
defending them against
prompt injection and data leaks.

31
00:02:09,896 --> 00:02:16,072
And part six takes everything to
production, with FastAPI, Docker, monitoring, and a

32
00:02:16,072 --> 00:02:19,381
full-stack AI
workspace as the final project.

33
00:02:20,681 --> 00:02:25,149
Every module follows the same
rhythm, so you always know where you are.

34
00:02:25,499 --> 00:02:30,453
First, the concepts, explained from
zero, with small examples you can run.

35
00:02:30,803 --> 00:02:33,471
Then a real project, built step by step.

36
00:02:33,691 --> 00:02:34,702
Not a teaser.

37
00:02:34,922 --> 00:02:38,733
Complete code, tests, and a
README you can run yourself.

38
00:02:39,083 --> 00:02:42,632
Next, the errors people
actually hit, and how to fix them.

39
00:02:42,982 --> 00:02:47,953
And finally, a recap, a short quiz
with explained answers, and an extension

40
00:02:47,953 --> 00:02:50,338
challenge if you want to go further.

41
00:02:51,638 --> 00:02:52,858
Now, Module 1.

42
00:02:53,078 --> 00:02:58,898
Agents are software, so before we touch
a language model, you need the Python that

43
00:02:58,898 --> 00:03:00,956
AI engineering actually uses.

44
00:03:01,306 --> 00:03:06,996
You will work with the core data types,
lists, dictionaries, and sets, in the shapes

45
00:03:06,996 --> 00:03:09,028
AI applications use every day.

46
00:03:09,378 --> 00:03:13,670
You will write functions and
classes, and handle errors with exceptions.

47
00:03:14,020 --> 00:03:19,966
You will organise code into modules and
packages, read and write files and JSON, and

48
00:03:19,966 --> 00:03:22,726
keep settings in environment variables.

49
00:03:23,076 --> 00:03:28,795
And you will add type hints, make HTTP
requests, and get a first, practical look at

50
00:03:28,795 --> 00:03:29,897
async and await.

51
00:03:31,197 --> 00:03:35,982
Everything comes together in this
module's project, the Python AI Toolkit.

52
00:03:36,202 --> 00:03:42,348
It is a command line tool that reads a
log of language model API calls, validates

53
00:03:42,348 --> 00:03:45,079
every record, and produces a report.

54
00:03:45,429 --> 00:03:46,871
Here is the finished tool.

55
00:03:47,091 --> 00:03:52,806
The validate command checks every record
and explains exactly what is wrong, like a

56
00:03:52,806 --> 00:03:55,422
negative token count, or a missing id.

57
00:03:55,772 --> 00:04:01,587
And the report command summarises a
clean log: requests, success rate, tokens,

58
00:04:01,587 --> 00:04:03,674
estimated cost, and latency.

59
00:04:03,894 --> 00:04:07,353
By the end of this module, you
will have written every line of it.

60
00:04:08,653 --> 00:04:13,712
To follow along you need a
computer running Windows, macOS, or Linux.

61
00:04:14,062 --> 00:04:16,690
You need Python 3.11 or newer.

62
00:04:16,910 --> 00:04:19,598
I am recording with Python 3.13.

63
00:04:19,948 --> 00:04:21,012
A code editor.

64
00:04:21,232 --> 00:04:25,829
VS Code with the official
Python extension is a good free choice.

65
00:04:26,179 --> 00:04:28,161
And no AI experience at all.

66
00:04:28,381 --> 00:04:33,450
If you have written a little code in any
language, that helps, but every concept is

67
00:04:33,450 --> 00:04:34,610
explained as we go.

68
00:04:35,910 --> 00:04:37,782
Let's set up a clean workspace.

69
00:04:38,002 --> 00:04:40,354
Open a terminal in an empty folder.

70
00:04:40,704 --> 00:04:42,518
First, check your Python version.

71
00:04:42,738 --> 00:04:46,356
On Windows the command is
usually just python, or py.

72
00:04:46,576 --> 00:04:49,160
Anything from 3.11 up is fine.

73
00:04:49,510 --> 00:04:51,668
Next, create a virtual environment.

74
00:04:51,888 --> 00:04:57,473
This makes a private folder, called dot
venv, with its own copy of Python and its

75
00:04:57,473 --> 00:04:59,059
own installed packages.

76
00:04:59,279 --> 00:05:04,530
Every project gets one, so the
libraries of one project can never break another.

77
00:05:04,880 --> 00:05:05,728
Activate it.

78
00:05:05,948 --> 00:05:11,313
On macOS and Linux, use source,
dot venv, slash bin, slash activate.

79
00:05:11,533 --> 00:05:15,974
On Windows, run dot venv,
backslash Scripts, backslash activate.

80
00:05:16,194 --> 00:05:19,884
Now, the python command
points inside your project folder.

81
00:05:20,234 --> 00:05:24,125
With the environment active,
pip installs packages into it.

82
00:05:24,345 --> 00:05:31,368
We install httpx, the HTTP library we
will use later in this module, and we pin the

83
00:05:31,368 --> 00:05:35,345
exact version, so that
your results match mine.

84
00:05:35,695 --> 00:05:40,956
Finally, pip freeze records the exact
versions you installed into a requirements

85
00:05:40,956 --> 00:05:41,285
file.

86
00:05:41,505 --> 00:05:47,507
Anyone can rebuild the same environment
with pip install, dash r, requirements dot

87
00:05:47,507 --> 00:05:47,873
text.

88
00:05:49,173 --> 00:05:50,137
One warning.

89
00:05:50,357 --> 00:05:55,730
If pip complains about an externally
managed environment, or packages seem to

90
00:05:55,730 --> 00:06:00,335
vanish, you are almost certainly
outside your virtual environment.

91
00:06:00,555 --> 00:06:05,895
Activate it again, and check that which
python, or where python on Windows, points

92
00:06:05,895 --> 00:06:07,523
into your project folder.

93
00:06:08,823 --> 00:06:13,567
This is the project layout we will
grow into by the end of the module.

94
00:06:13,917 --> 00:06:18,944
At the top: the virtual environment,
which is never committed to git, a settings

95
00:06:18,944 --> 00:06:21,331
template, and the pinned requirements.

96
00:06:21,681 --> 00:06:25,552
Configuration and sample
data live in their own folders.

97
00:06:25,902 --> 00:06:28,978
The real code lives in
a package called toolkit.

98
00:06:29,198 --> 00:06:34,232
The special file dunder main lets us
run the whole package with python dash m

99
00:06:34,232 --> 00:06:34,755
toolkit.

100
00:06:35,105 --> 00:06:37,080
And tests live in their own folder.

101
00:06:37,300 --> 00:06:43,226
Keeping code, data, configuration, and
tests separate is a habit that pays off in

102
00:06:43,226 --> 00:06:45,201
every AI project you build.

103
00:06:46,501 --> 00:06:52,190
An agent is a program that decides,
acts, observes, and repeats, working towards a

104
00:06:52,190 --> 00:06:52,537
goal.

105
00:06:52,887 --> 00:06:54,558
The model only decides.

106
00:06:54,778 --> 00:06:57,171
Your Python code performs the actions.

107
00:06:57,521 --> 00:07:02,090
Every project gets its own
virtual environment, with pinned versions.

108
00:07:02,440 --> 00:07:08,058
In the next lesson, we write real
Python: the data types that every AI application

109
00:07:08,058 --> 00:07:09,017
is built from.

110
00:07:09,237 --> 00:07:10,100
See you there.

111
00:07:11,420 --> 00:07:17,511
Every AI application, from a simple
chatbot to a team of agents, moves the same few

112
00:07:17,511 --> 00:07:19,052
kinds of data around.

113
00:07:19,272 --> 00:07:23,297
Text, numbers, lists of
messages, and dictionaries of settings.

114
00:07:23,517 --> 00:07:29,128
In this lesson you will learn those
building blocks properly, using the exact shapes

115
00:07:29,128 --> 00:07:31,065
that language model APIs use.

116
00:07:32,365 --> 00:07:36,454
You will create variables and
recognise Python's basic types.

117
00:07:36,804 --> 00:07:39,018
Format text cleanly with f-strings.

118
00:07:39,368 --> 00:07:43,225
Use lists, dictionaries, and
sets, and know when to pick each one.

119
00:07:43,575 --> 00:07:49,018
And build a chat conversation as a list
of dictionaries, which is exactly the format

120
00:07:49,018 --> 00:07:51,415
you will send to a model in Module 2.

121
00:07:52,715 --> 00:07:55,379
A variable is a name
that points at a value.

122
00:07:55,599 --> 00:07:59,664
Every value has a type, and the
type decides what you can do with it.

123
00:08:00,014 --> 00:08:02,257
A string, s t r, holds text.

124
00:08:02,477 --> 00:08:05,598
Prompts, model names,
and replies are all strings.

125
00:08:05,948 --> 00:08:08,954
An int is a whole
number, like a token count.

126
00:08:09,304 --> 00:08:13,591
A float is a decimal number,
like a temperature setting or a price.

127
00:08:13,941 --> 00:08:17,799
A bool is either True or
False, with a capital T and F.

128
00:08:18,149 --> 00:08:20,034
And None means no value at all.

129
00:08:20,254 --> 00:08:24,492
It's often used for something
that is optional, or not loaded yet.

130
00:08:25,792 --> 00:08:27,536
Here they are in a small script.

131
00:08:27,886 --> 00:08:30,987
Each line creates a
variable with the equals sign.

132
00:08:31,207 --> 00:08:33,309
You do not declare the type yourself.

133
00:08:33,529 --> 00:08:35,480
Python works it out from the value.

134
00:08:35,830 --> 00:08:39,499
The built-in type function
tells you what kind of value you have.

135
00:08:39,719 --> 00:08:43,313
That is very handy when
something behaves unexpectedly.

136
00:08:43,663 --> 00:08:45,008
This is an f-string.

137
00:08:45,228 --> 00:08:50,862
Put the letter f before the quotes, and
anything inside curly braces is evaluated

138
00:08:50,862 --> 00:08:52,740
and inserted into the text.

139
00:08:52,960 --> 00:08:55,438
You will build
prompts this way all the time.

140
00:08:55,788 --> 00:08:58,893
A name can be pointed
at a new value at any time.

141
00:08:59,113 --> 00:09:01,373
And f-strings can format values too.

142
00:09:01,593 --> 00:09:04,183
Colon comma adds thousands separators.

143
00:09:05,483 --> 00:09:10,487
Run it, and you see the three types,
the formatted sentence, and the new limit of

144
00:09:10,487 --> 00:09:11,723
two thousand tokens.

145
00:09:12,073 --> 00:09:13,531
Now a classic mistake.

146
00:09:13,751 --> 00:09:18,951
Joining a string and a number with plus
fails with a type error, because Python will

147
00:09:18,951 --> 00:09:21,675
not guess how to
combine text with a number.

148
00:09:22,025 --> 00:09:26,425
The fix is an f-string, or
converting the number with s t r first.

149
00:09:26,645 --> 00:09:28,932
Read the last line of any error first.

150
00:09:29,152 --> 00:09:31,004
It names the problem precisely.

151
00:09:32,304 --> 00:09:33,300
Next, lists.

152
00:09:33,520 --> 00:09:37,711
A list keeps items in order, and
you can change it after creating it.

153
00:09:38,061 --> 00:09:39,778
Square brackets create a list.

154
00:09:39,998 --> 00:09:41,808
Here, three user messages.

155
00:09:42,158 --> 00:09:45,371
You read an item by its
position, called its index.

156
00:09:45,591 --> 00:09:51,166
Indexes start at zero, so zero is the
first item, and minus one is the last.

157
00:09:51,386 --> 00:09:54,373
The len function gives
you the number of items.

158
00:09:54,723 --> 00:09:56,230
Append adds to the end.

159
00:09:56,450 --> 00:09:57,983
A slice takes a range.

160
00:09:58,203 --> 00:10:03,016
One colon three means start at
index one and stop before index three.

161
00:10:03,366 --> 00:10:05,199
A for loop visits every item.

162
00:10:05,419 --> 00:10:10,580
Wrapping the list in enumerate gives you
a running number as well, starting wherever

163
00:10:10,580 --> 00:10:11,256
you choose.

164
00:10:12,556 --> 00:10:17,687
The output shows the first and last
message, the count of three, the slice, and then

165
00:10:17,687 --> 00:10:20,191
the numbered list after Bye was appended.

166
00:10:21,491 --> 00:10:25,213
Dictionaries are the most
important structure in this whole course.

167
00:10:25,433 --> 00:10:30,397
A dictionary maps keys to values, so
you look things up by name instead of by

168
00:10:30,397 --> 00:10:30,977
position.

169
00:10:31,327 --> 00:10:33,466
This dictionary is a chat message.

170
00:10:33,686 --> 00:10:37,948
It has a role, saying who is
speaking, and the content, which is the text.

171
00:10:38,168 --> 00:10:40,896
This is the same shape real chat APIs use.

172
00:10:41,246 --> 00:10:45,851
Square brackets with a key read a
value, and assigning to a new key adds it.

173
00:10:46,201 --> 00:10:48,615
The get method is the safe way to read.

174
00:10:48,835 --> 00:10:52,544
If the key is missing, you get
the default instead of an error.

175
00:10:52,894 --> 00:10:54,230
Now combine the two.

176
00:10:54,450 --> 00:10:59,771
A list of message dictionaries is a
whole conversation: a system instruction, the

177
00:10:59,771 --> 00:11:02,661
user's question, and
the assistant's answer.

178
00:11:02,881 --> 00:11:07,569
When you call a model in Module 2,
this is precisely what you will send.

179
00:11:07,919 --> 00:11:13,486
Looping over it, the f-string pads each
role to nine characters, so the output lines

180
00:11:13,486 --> 00:11:14,149
up neatly.

181
00:11:15,449 --> 00:11:19,710
Here is the result, including
the aligned conversation at the end.

182
00:11:20,060 --> 00:11:23,624
And here is the error you
will meet most often: a key error.

183
00:11:23,844 --> 00:11:26,930
The key content does not
exist in this dictionary.

184
00:11:27,280 --> 00:11:30,971
When a key might be missing,
use get with a sensible default.

185
00:11:31,191 --> 00:11:35,046
When it must exist, check it
explicitly and give a clear message.

186
00:11:35,266 --> 00:11:38,191
You will do exactly that
in this module's project.

187
00:11:39,491 --> 00:11:41,212
The third collection is the set.

188
00:11:41,432 --> 00:11:46,175
A set holds unique items, with no
duplicates and no particular order.

189
00:11:46,525 --> 00:11:50,267
Turning a list of tags into
a set removes the duplicates.

190
00:11:50,487 --> 00:11:55,539
Because sets have no order, we sort the
set before printing it, so the output is

191
00:11:55,539 --> 00:11:56,297
predictable.

192
00:11:56,647 --> 00:12:02,049
The in operator checks membership, and
on a set it stays fast however large the set

193
00:12:02,049 --> 00:12:02,440
grows.

194
00:12:02,790 --> 00:12:05,651
Sets also support maths-like operations.

195
00:12:05,871 --> 00:12:11,417
Here, the tools an agent requested,
minus the tools it is allowed to use, gives

196
00:12:11,417 --> 00:12:13,524
exactly the forbidden request.

197
00:12:13,744 --> 00:12:15,401
Keep this idea in mind.

198
00:12:15,621 --> 00:12:18,568
Tool allowlists come
back in the security module.

199
00:12:19,868 --> 00:12:25,252
Three unique tags, membership is True,
and delete files is flagged as not allowed.

200
00:12:26,552 --> 00:12:29,634
One last tool for this
lesson: comprehensions.

201
00:12:29,854 --> 00:12:34,678
They build a new list or dictionary
from an existing collection, in a single

202
00:12:34,678 --> 00:12:35,566
readable line.

203
00:12:35,916 --> 00:12:41,117
Here are three request records, a list
of dictionaries, just like a usage log.

204
00:12:41,467 --> 00:12:44,553
This list comprehension
reads almost like English.

205
00:12:44,773 --> 00:12:49,367
Take r's id, for each r in
records, if r's status is ok.

206
00:12:49,717 --> 00:12:55,933
Curly braces with a key, colon, value
build a dictionary instead, here mapping each

207
00:12:55,933 --> 00:12:57,206
id to its tokens.

208
00:12:57,556 --> 00:13:02,350
And without any brackets, the same
pattern can feed straight into a function like

209
00:13:02,350 --> 00:13:02,586
sum.

210
00:13:03,886 --> 00:13:09,623
Two successful ids, a dictionary of
tokens, and eight hundred and fifty tokens in

211
00:13:09,623 --> 00:13:10,048
total.

212
00:13:11,348 --> 00:13:16,955
Use a list when order matters, like the
messages in a conversation, or the steps of

213
00:13:16,955 --> 00:13:17,427
a plan.

214
00:13:17,777 --> 00:13:23,530
Use a dictionary when you look things up
by name, like a message, a configuration,

215
00:13:23,530 --> 00:13:25,564
or a record from a JSON file.

216
00:13:25,914 --> 00:13:31,196
Use a set for uniqueness and fast
membership checks, like allowed tools or IDs

217
00:13:31,196 --> 00:13:32,889
you've already processed.

218
00:13:33,239 --> 00:13:38,325
And a tuple, written with parentheses,
is a fixed group of values that will not

219
00:13:38,325 --> 00:13:40,643
change, such as a tag and its count.

220
00:13:41,943 --> 00:13:42,751
Quick check.

221
00:13:42,971 --> 00:13:44,404
What does this code print?

222
00:13:44,624 --> 00:13:46,682
Pause the video if you need a moment.

223
00:13:52,382 --> 00:13:53,852
The answer is unknown.

224
00:13:54,072 --> 00:13:57,322
The get method returns the
default when the key is missing.

225
00:13:57,542 --> 00:14:00,488
Only square-bracket
access would raise a key error.

226
00:14:01,788 --> 00:14:07,042
Variables are names pointing at values,
and the type function tells you what a value

227
00:14:07,042 --> 00:14:07,229
is.

228
00:14:07,579 --> 00:14:12,202
F-strings are the clean way to build
text, and they can format numbers too.

229
00:14:12,552 --> 00:14:17,173
Lists keep order, dictionaries look
up by key, and sets keep things unique.

230
00:14:17,523 --> 00:14:22,379
And a conversation is a list of
dictionaries, each with a role and some content.

231
00:14:22,729 --> 00:14:28,771
Next lesson: functions, classes, and
exceptions, so you can organise this data into

232
00:14:28,771 --> 00:14:30,518
reusable, reliable code.

233
00:14:31,825 --> 00:14:34,824
In the last lesson you
learned how to hold data.

234
00:14:35,044 --> 00:14:38,569
Now you will learn how to
organise the code that works on it.

235
00:14:38,789 --> 00:14:44,407
Functions give logic a name, classes
bundle data with behaviour, and exceptions let

236
00:14:44,407 --> 00:14:47,453
your program fail
clearly and recover safely.

237
00:14:47,673 --> 00:14:52,675
Agents call tools that fail all the
time, so this last part matters a lot.

238
00:14:53,975 --> 00:14:58,819
You will write functions with
parameters, default values, and return values.

239
00:14:59,169 --> 00:15:02,849
Avoid a classic bug that
can leak data between users.

240
00:15:03,199 --> 00:15:06,528
Create your own classes,
and the shorter dataclasses.

241
00:15:06,878 --> 00:15:11,810
And handle, raise, and define your own
exceptions, and read a traceback calmly.

242
00:15:13,110 --> 00:15:16,431
A function is a named,
reusable block of code.

243
00:15:16,651 --> 00:15:19,912
You define it once, and
call it as often as you like.

244
00:15:20,262 --> 00:15:22,185
The def keyword starts a function.

245
00:15:22,405 --> 00:15:25,870
Inside the parentheses are
its parameters, the inputs.

246
00:15:26,090 --> 00:15:30,160
The text in triple quotes is a
docstring, describing what it does.

247
00:15:30,380 --> 00:15:33,418
And return sends the
result back to whoever called it.

248
00:15:33,768 --> 00:15:38,557
You can pass arguments by
position, or by name, as keyword arguments.

249
00:15:38,777 --> 00:15:42,394
Keywords make calls with
several numbers much easier to read.

250
00:15:42,614 --> 00:15:44,769
Notice the underscores in two million.

251
00:15:44,989 --> 00:15:46,287
Python ignores them.

252
00:15:46,507 --> 00:15:48,319
They are just there for readability.

253
00:15:48,669 --> 00:15:51,415
This second function has a default value.

254
00:15:51,635 --> 00:15:55,045
If the caller does not
pass max words, it uses eight.

255
00:15:55,265 --> 00:15:58,294
It returns early when the
text is already short enough.

256
00:15:58,514 --> 00:16:01,957
Otherwise it keeps the
first words and adds an ellipsis.

257
00:16:02,307 --> 00:16:07,805
Here we call it three times: once with a
long sentence, once with a short one, and

258
00:16:07,805 --> 00:16:09,682
once overriding the default.

259
00:16:10,982 --> 00:16:16,379
The costs come back as plain numbers,
the long sentence is shortened, and the last

260
00:16:16,379 --> 00:16:18,091
call keeps only two words.

261
00:16:19,391 --> 00:16:24,639
Give each function one job, and name
it with a verb, like estimate cost or load

262
00:16:24,639 --> 00:16:25,170
records.

263
00:16:25,520 --> 00:16:27,916
Return results instead of printing them.

264
00:16:28,136 --> 00:16:32,620
Then the caller decides what to do,
and you can test the function easily.

265
00:16:32,970 --> 00:16:38,384
Use keyword arguments whenever a call
would otherwise be a mystery list of numbers.

266
00:16:38,734 --> 00:16:42,185
And never use a mutable
default, like an empty list.

267
00:16:42,405 --> 00:16:43,628
Let me show you why.

268
00:16:44,928 --> 00:16:46,820
This function stores chat history.

269
00:16:47,040 --> 00:16:52,432
The default empty list looks harmless,
but Python creates it only once, when the

270
00:16:52,432 --> 00:16:53,781
function is defined.

271
00:16:54,001 --> 00:16:57,446
Every call that uses the
default shares that same list.

272
00:16:57,796 --> 00:17:03,477
So Alice sends a message, then Bob
sends one, and Bob's history contains Alice's

273
00:17:03,477 --> 00:17:04,045
message.

274
00:17:04,265 --> 00:17:10,229
In an AI app, that is a privacy bug: one
user's conversation leaking into another's.

275
00:17:10,579 --> 00:17:13,051
The fix is the standard Python pattern.

276
00:17:13,271 --> 00:17:17,013
Default to None, and create a
fresh list inside the function.

277
00:17:18,313 --> 00:17:19,564
The output proves it.

278
00:17:19,784 --> 00:17:22,943
With the buggy version,
Bob sees Alice's message.

279
00:17:23,163 --> 00:17:25,977
With the fixed
version, each call starts clean.

280
00:17:27,277 --> 00:17:28,365
Now, classes.

281
00:17:28,585 --> 00:17:30,581
Functions organise behaviour.

282
00:17:30,801 --> 00:17:35,165
Classes organise data together
with the behaviour that belongs to it.

283
00:17:35,515 --> 00:17:37,021
A class is a blueprint.

284
00:17:37,241 --> 00:17:41,578
It describes what every object of
that kind has, and what it can do.

285
00:17:41,928 --> 00:17:47,322
An object, also called an instance, is
one real thing built from that blueprint.

286
00:17:47,542 --> 00:17:49,671
You can create as many as you like.

287
00:17:50,021 --> 00:17:52,986
Attributes are the
data stored on each object.

288
00:17:53,336 --> 00:17:58,421
And methods are functions that belong to
the class and work on that object's data.

289
00:17:59,721 --> 00:18:01,681
Here is a Conversation class.

290
00:18:01,901 --> 00:18:06,470
The special dunder init method
runs whenever a new object is created.

291
00:18:06,690 --> 00:18:12,556
Its first parameter, self, is the object
being built, so self dot messages stores a

292
00:18:12,556 --> 00:18:14,746
list on that particular object.

293
00:18:15,096 --> 00:18:16,705
Add and last are methods.

294
00:18:16,925 --> 00:18:22,096
They also take self first, so they can
read and change this object's messages.

295
00:18:22,446 --> 00:18:26,683
Methods with double underscores
plug your class into Python itself.

296
00:18:26,903 --> 00:18:31,751
Defining dunder len makes the
built-in len function work on a conversation.

297
00:18:32,101 --> 00:18:34,889
Creating an object
looks like calling a function.

298
00:18:35,109 --> 00:18:38,928
We add two messages, then print
the length and the last message.

299
00:18:39,278 --> 00:18:43,754
A second conversation is a
completely separate object, with its own list.

300
00:18:45,054 --> 00:18:50,057
Three messages in the first
conversation, and just the system message in the other.

301
00:18:51,357 --> 00:18:58,123
Many classes in AI code mostly hold
data: a model configuration, a usage record, a

302
00:18:58,123 --> 00:18:59,113
tool result.

303
00:18:59,333 --> 00:19:04,215
For those, Python's dataclass
decorator writes the boring parts for you.

304
00:19:04,565 --> 00:19:08,353
You just list the fields, with
their types and optional defaults.

305
00:19:08,573 --> 00:19:13,635
The decorator generates the init
method, a readable print form, and equality

306
00:19:13,635 --> 00:19:14,368
comparison.

307
00:19:14,718 --> 00:19:16,243
Notice the stop field.

308
00:19:16,463 --> 00:19:18,679
Remember the mutable default bug?

309
00:19:18,899 --> 00:19:24,116
Dataclasses protect you from it: for a
list default, you must use field with a

310
00:19:24,116 --> 00:19:25,186
default factory.

311
00:19:25,536 --> 00:19:30,940
Creating objects, printing one, and
comparing two with the same values, which counts

312
00:19:30,940 --> 00:19:31,519
as equal.

313
00:19:32,819 --> 00:19:37,659
The generated print form shows every
field, and the equality check is True.

314
00:19:37,879 --> 00:19:41,224
In the project, our
usage record is a dataclass.

315
00:19:42,524 --> 00:19:43,624
Now, exceptions.

316
00:19:43,844 --> 00:19:47,118
When something goes wrong,
Python raises an exception.

317
00:19:47,338 --> 00:19:49,829
If nothing handles it, the program stops.

318
00:19:50,179 --> 00:19:52,915
Put code that might
fail inside a try block.

319
00:19:53,135 --> 00:19:56,785
Converting text to a number
fails if the text isn't a number.

320
00:19:57,135 --> 00:20:00,734
The except block runs only
if that specific error happens.

321
00:20:00,954 --> 00:20:05,084
Here we handle a value error by
reporting it and returning zero.

322
00:20:05,434 --> 00:20:08,760
The else block runs only
when the try block succeeded.

323
00:20:09,110 --> 00:20:12,830
And finally always runs,
whether things went well or not.

324
00:20:13,050 --> 00:20:16,785
That makes it the right place for
cleanup, like closing a connection.

325
00:20:18,085 --> 00:20:19,191
Look at the order.

326
00:20:19,411 --> 00:20:24,781
For one twenty, the finally message
prints before the value, because finally runs on

327
00:20:24,781 --> 00:20:26,571
the way out of the function.

328
00:20:26,791 --> 00:20:31,581
For a b c, the error is handled,
finally still runs, and we get zero.

329
00:20:32,881 --> 00:20:36,750
You can define your own
exceptions by inheriting from Exception.

330
00:20:36,970 --> 00:20:42,135
A base class for your project, with more
specific ones below it, is a great pattern.

331
00:20:42,485 --> 00:20:47,213
The raise statement stops the function
immediately and hands the problem to the

332
00:20:47,213 --> 00:20:47,632
caller.

333
00:20:47,852 --> 00:20:51,840
A negative cost is a
programming error, so that's a value error.

334
00:20:52,060 --> 00:20:57,126
Going over budget is a business rule,
so it gets our own budget exceeded error.

335
00:20:57,476 --> 00:21:01,372
The caller catches only
budget exceeded, and stops charging.

336
00:21:01,592 --> 00:21:07,121
Agents need exactly this kind of guard,
a hard spending limit, which we'll build

337
00:21:07,121 --> 00:21:08,572
properly in Module 7.

338
00:21:09,872 --> 00:21:14,762
The first two charges succeed, and
the third is refused with a clear message.

339
00:21:16,062 --> 00:21:18,391
On the left is a pattern you must avoid.

340
00:21:18,611 --> 00:21:23,453
A bare except with pass hides every
error, including bugs in your own code.

341
00:21:23,673 --> 00:21:27,980
The program carries on with no
result, and you have no idea why.

342
00:21:28,330 --> 00:21:32,539
On the right, you catch only the
error you expect and know how to handle.

343
00:21:32,759 --> 00:21:36,965
Anything unexpected still
surfaces, so you can see it and fix it.

344
00:21:38,265 --> 00:21:41,779
When an exception isn't
handled, Python prints a traceback.

345
00:21:41,999 --> 00:21:44,532
It looks scary, but
read it from the bottom up.

346
00:21:44,752 --> 00:21:48,423
The last line is the error:
a key error for the key model.

347
00:21:48,773 --> 00:21:53,910
The lines above it show the path the
program took, with the most recent call last.

348
00:21:54,130 --> 00:21:59,734
Here, the failing line is config, model,
inside get model, called from start agent.

349
00:21:59,954 --> 00:22:05,185
So the fix is to make sure the
configuration contains a model, or to read it with

350
00:22:05,185 --> 00:22:06,348
get and a default.

351
00:22:07,648 --> 00:22:08,456
Quick check.

352
00:22:08,676 --> 00:22:13,808
Why is a function with history
equals an empty list as a default dangerous?

353
00:22:19,508 --> 00:22:24,372
Because the default list is created
once and shared by every call that uses it.

354
00:22:24,592 --> 00:22:28,302
Default to None and create a
fresh list inside the function.

355
00:22:29,602 --> 00:22:33,849
Functions do one job, have a
clear name, and return their result.

356
00:22:34,199 --> 00:22:36,267
Default mutable arguments to None.

357
00:22:36,617 --> 00:22:41,998
Classes bundle data and behaviour, and
dataclasses make data-heavy classes short.

358
00:22:42,348 --> 00:22:47,240
Catch specific exceptions, raise
meaningful ones, and read tracebacks from the

359
00:22:47,240 --> 00:22:47,867
bottom up.

360
00:22:48,217 --> 00:22:54,020
Next, you will split code across files
and packages, and work with files, JSON, and

361
00:22:54,020 --> 00:22:55,559
environment variables.

362
00:22:56,870 --> 00:22:59,918
Real AI projects are never a single file.

363
00:23:00,138 --> 00:23:06,159
They read prompts and documents from
disk, exchange JSON with APIs, and need secret

364
00:23:06,159 --> 00:23:09,422
API keys that must
never end up in your code.

365
00:23:09,642 --> 00:23:11,441
This lesson covers all of that.

366
00:23:12,741 --> 00:23:17,267
You will split code into modules
and packages, and import between them.

367
00:23:17,617 --> 00:23:20,115
Read and write files safely with pathlib.

368
00:23:20,465 --> 00:23:25,183
Convert between JSON and Python
objects, and handle invalid JSON.

369
00:23:25,533 --> 00:23:29,659
And keep configuration and
secrets in environment variables.

370
00:23:30,959 --> 00:23:33,365
First, how Python code is organised.

371
00:23:33,715 --> 00:23:35,953
A module is simply a Python file.

372
00:23:36,173 --> 00:23:41,878
Anything defined in cleaning dot py,
functions, classes, variables, can be imported

373
00:23:41,878 --> 00:23:42,566
elsewhere.

374
00:23:42,916 --> 00:23:48,034
A package is a folder of modules
with a file called dunder init dot py.

375
00:23:48,254 --> 00:23:53,862
That file runs when the package is
imported, and it often re-exports the most useful

376
00:23:53,862 --> 00:23:54,263
names.

377
00:23:54,613 --> 00:24:00,013
Your program imports the package by
name, exactly like it imports anything else.

378
00:24:00,363 --> 00:24:06,688
And there are two other sources of
modules: the standard library, which ships with

379
00:24:06,688 --> 00:24:13,012
Python, like json, pathlib, and os, and
third-party packages you install with pip,

380
00:24:13,012 --> 00:24:13,860
like httpx.

381
00:24:15,160 --> 00:24:16,475
Here is the module.

382
00:24:16,695 --> 00:24:22,337
Normalize collapses runs of whitespace
by splitting the text into words and joining

383
00:24:22,337 --> 00:24:24,309
them back with single spaces.

384
00:24:24,659 --> 00:24:26,965
And word count reuses normalize.

385
00:24:27,185 --> 00:24:29,478
Small functions that build on each other.

386
00:24:30,778 --> 00:24:36,230
The package's init file imports the two
functions, so users can call them directly

387
00:24:36,230 --> 00:24:36,962
on textkit.

388
00:24:37,312 --> 00:24:40,174
And dunder all
documents which names are public.

389
00:24:41,474 --> 00:24:42,805
Two import styles.

390
00:24:43,025 --> 00:24:47,007
Import textkit gives you the
whole package, used with a prefix.

391
00:24:47,227 --> 00:24:51,616
From textkit dot cleaning import
word count brings in a single name.

392
00:24:51,966 --> 00:24:53,810
The main function does the work.

393
00:24:54,160 --> 00:24:56,552
This final check is a Python idiom.

394
00:24:56,772 --> 00:25:01,823
Dunder name equals dunder main is
true only when the file is run directly.

395
00:25:02,043 --> 00:25:06,037
If another file imports it,
main does not run automatically.

396
00:25:07,337 --> 00:25:10,634
Running main gives the
cleaned text and the word count.

397
00:25:10,984 --> 00:25:16,073
And because textkit is a proper package,
any other code in this folder can import it

398
00:25:16,073 --> 00:25:16,315
too.

399
00:25:17,615 --> 00:25:22,455
If you see module not found error for
your own package, you are almost always

400
00:25:22,455 --> 00:25:24,781
running Python from the wrong folder.

401
00:25:25,001 --> 00:25:28,867
Python looks for imports starting
in the folder of the script you run.

402
00:25:29,087 --> 00:25:33,222
Run from the project root, and
for packages, use python dash m.

403
00:25:34,522 --> 00:25:35,594
Next, files.

404
00:25:35,814 --> 00:25:39,659
Prompts, documents, logs,
and reports all live in files.

405
00:25:39,879 --> 00:25:42,440
The modern way to
work with them is pathlib.

406
00:25:42,790 --> 00:25:45,364
A Path object represents a file path.

407
00:25:45,584 --> 00:25:48,140
Write text creates or overwrites the file.

408
00:25:48,360 --> 00:25:51,168
Always pass encoding equals utf eight.

409
00:25:51,388 --> 00:25:56,715
The default encoding is different on
Windows, and AI text is full of characters

410
00:25:56,715 --> 00:25:58,198
outside basic English.

411
00:25:58,548 --> 00:26:04,101
Read text returns the whole file as one
string, and splitlines turns it into a list

412
00:26:04,101 --> 00:26:04,703
of lines.

413
00:26:05,053 --> 00:26:08,432
For more control, open
the file inside a with block.

414
00:26:08,652 --> 00:26:11,477
The mode a means
append, so we add to the end.

415
00:26:11,697 --> 00:26:16,353
The with statement guarantees the
file is closed, even if something fails.

416
00:26:16,703 --> 00:26:21,849
Finally, number the lines, and ask the
path a few questions: does it exist, what is

417
00:26:21,849 --> 00:26:24,143
its suffix, and how many bytes is it.

418
00:26:25,443 --> 00:26:29,958
Two lines written, a third
appended, and the file is thirty-four bytes.

419
00:26:31,258 --> 00:26:36,260
JSON is a text format for data,
and it is everywhere in AI engineering.

420
00:26:36,480 --> 00:26:43,041
Model APIs send and receive JSON, tool
calls are described in JSON, and logs are

421
00:26:43,041 --> 00:26:43,943
often JSON.

422
00:26:44,293 --> 00:26:46,599
JSON looks very similar to Python.

423
00:26:46,819 --> 00:26:52,262
Objects in curly braces, arrays in
square brackets, strings, numbers, true, false,

424
00:26:52,262 --> 00:26:52,859
and null.

425
00:26:53,209 --> 00:26:56,320
Python's json module maps them directly.

426
00:26:56,540 --> 00:27:01,758
Objects become dictionaries, arrays
become lists, and true, false, and null become

427
00:27:01,758 --> 00:27:03,158
True, False, and None.

428
00:27:03,378 --> 00:27:05,354
But JSON is stricter than Python.

429
00:27:05,574 --> 00:27:09,503
Strings need double quotes, and
trailing commas are not allowed.

430
00:27:10,803 --> 00:27:13,673
We start from an
ordinary Python dictionary.

431
00:27:14,023 --> 00:27:19,227
json dot dumps, with an s for
string, converts Python into JSON text.

432
00:27:19,447 --> 00:27:21,203
Indent two makes it readable.

433
00:27:21,423 --> 00:27:22,892
Then we save it to a file.

434
00:27:23,242 --> 00:27:29,532
json dot loads does the reverse, turning
JSON text back into Python objects, so the

435
00:27:29,532 --> 00:27:31,881
tokens are a real number again.

436
00:27:32,231 --> 00:27:35,789
And invalid JSON
raises a JSON decode error.

437
00:27:36,009 --> 00:27:37,483
Here, a trailing comma.

438
00:27:37,703 --> 00:27:43,073
Language models sometimes produce
slightly broken JSON, so you will handle this

439
00:27:43,073 --> 00:27:43,889
error a lot.

440
00:27:45,189 --> 00:27:50,620
Notice true and null in the JSON text,
the token count plus one, and the precise

441
00:27:50,620 --> 00:27:53,810
error message, which
even tells you the column.

442
00:27:55,110 --> 00:27:58,244
Environment variables
keep secrets out of your code.

443
00:27:58,464 --> 00:28:04,266
An API key written in a source file ends
up in git history, in screenshots, and in

444
00:28:04,266 --> 00:28:04,620
logs.

445
00:28:04,970 --> 00:28:09,938
They also let settings change without
editing code, such as a different model on

446
00:28:09,938 --> 00:28:11,801
your laptop and in production.

447
00:28:12,151 --> 00:28:17,527
And every platform supports them:
your terminal, Docker, cloud hosts, and CI

448
00:28:17,527 --> 00:28:18,234
pipelines.

449
00:28:18,454 --> 00:28:21,405
You'll use them all the way to Module 17.

450
00:28:22,705 --> 00:28:28,858
The os module exposes environment
variables as a dictionary-like object called

451
00:28:28,858 --> 00:28:29,489
environ.

452
00:28:29,709 --> 00:28:34,100
Get returns None if the variable
isn't set, or a default you choose.

453
00:28:34,450 --> 00:28:39,604
If the key is missing, say so clearly
instead of failing later in a confusing way.

454
00:28:39,824 --> 00:28:41,699
And never print the secret itself.

455
00:28:41,919 --> 00:28:44,817
Printing its length is
enough to confirm it loaded.

456
00:28:46,117 --> 00:28:51,196
With nothing set, the script refuses
to continue and uses the default model.

457
00:28:51,546 --> 00:28:57,014
On macOS or Linux, you can set
variables for a single command like this.

458
00:28:57,234 --> 00:28:59,511
This is a fake key, just for the demo.

459
00:28:59,731 --> 00:29:05,555
On Windows PowerShell, you'd first run
dollar env, colon, DEMO API KEY, equals, and

460
00:29:05,555 --> 00:29:06,959
the value in quotes.

461
00:29:08,259 --> 00:29:13,888
Typing variables every time gets tedious,
so projects keep them in a file called dot

462
00:29:13,888 --> 00:29:14,156
env.

463
00:29:14,376 --> 00:29:19,767
You commit a template called dot env dot
example, with fake values, so others know

464
00:29:19,767 --> 00:29:20,556
what to set.

465
00:29:20,906 --> 00:29:26,933
The real dot env file holds your actual
keys, and it is listed in dot gitignore, so

466
00:29:26,933 --> 00:29:28,458
git never records it.

467
00:29:28,678 --> 00:29:33,807
In the project, we'll write a tiny
loader that reads dot env into the environment.

468
00:29:34,027 --> 00:29:36,773
Real variables always win over the file.

469
00:29:38,073 --> 00:29:38,881
Quick check.

470
00:29:39,101 --> 00:29:42,817
Which call turns this JSON
text into a Python dictionary?

471
00:29:48,517 --> 00:29:50,042
json dot loads.

472
00:29:50,262 --> 00:29:51,734
The s stands for string.

473
00:29:51,954 --> 00:29:57,344
Dumps goes the other way, and load
without an s reads from an open file object.

474
00:29:58,644 --> 00:30:03,402
A module is a file, and a package
is a folder with a dunder init file.

475
00:30:03,752 --> 00:30:07,303
Use pathlib, and always
set the encoding to utf eight.

476
00:30:07,653 --> 00:30:12,292
Convert with json loads and
dumps, and catch JSON decode errors.

477
00:30:12,642 --> 00:30:17,846
Secrets live in environment variables,
and only the example file is committed.

478
00:30:18,196 --> 00:30:24,996
In the next lesson, you'll add type
hints, talk to web APIs over HTTP, and see why

479
00:30:24,996 --> 00:30:27,235
async makes AI apps faster.

480
00:30:28,539 --> 00:30:33,683
Every language model you will use
in this course lives behind a web API.

481
00:30:33,903 --> 00:30:37,854
You send an HTTP request,
and you wait for the answer.

482
00:30:38,074 --> 00:30:44,484
This lesson gives you the three tools
that make that work well: type hints, HTTP

483
00:30:44,484 --> 00:30:47,768
requests with httpx, and async and await.

484
00:30:49,068 --> 00:30:53,569
You will annotate functions with type
hints, and check them with a type checker.

485
00:30:53,919 --> 00:30:58,492
Understand HTTP requests,
responses, and status codes.

486
00:30:58,842 --> 00:31:04,041
Call web APIs with httpx,
including timeouts and error handling.

487
00:31:04,391 --> 00:31:08,184
And run slow operations at the
same time with async and await.

488
00:31:09,484 --> 00:31:12,937
A type hint is a label
saying what type a value should be.

489
00:31:13,157 --> 00:31:18,336
After each parameter you write a colon
and a type, and after the parentheses an

490
00:31:18,336 --> 00:31:20,040
arrow and the return type.

491
00:31:20,260 --> 00:31:23,636
This function takes a list
of floats and returns a float.

492
00:31:23,986 --> 00:31:28,538
Here, the dictionary maps strings to
floats, and the vertical bar means or.

493
00:31:28,758 --> 00:31:32,728
The function returns a float,
or None when the model isn't found.

494
00:31:33,078 --> 00:31:36,072
Called correctly,
everything works as you'd expect.

495
00:31:36,422 --> 00:31:38,102
But here's the important part.

496
00:31:38,322 --> 00:31:41,104
Python does not enforce
hints when the code runs.

497
00:31:41,324 --> 00:31:45,863
Passing a string is allowed, and it
only crashes deep inside the function.

498
00:31:47,163 --> 00:31:52,749
Running it: two good results, and then a
confusing type error from inside sum, far

499
00:31:52,749 --> 00:31:54,588
away from the real mistake.

500
00:31:54,938 --> 00:31:59,973
A type checker like mypy reads your
hints without running the code, and points at

501
00:31:59,973 --> 00:32:03,889
the exact line with the wrong
argument, before you ever run it.

502
00:32:04,109 --> 00:32:07,350
Your editor uses the
same hints for autocompletion.

503
00:32:08,650 --> 00:32:11,957
The simple types:
str, int, float, and bool.

504
00:32:12,307 --> 00:32:17,607
Collections say what's inside: a list
of strings, or a dictionary from strings to

505
00:32:17,607 --> 00:32:17,934
ints.

506
00:32:18,284 --> 00:32:21,803
A vertical bar with None
means the value might be missing.

507
00:32:22,153 --> 00:32:25,938
A tuple hint lists each
position, like a string and an int.

508
00:32:26,288 --> 00:32:32,538
In Module 3, a library called Pydantic
will use exactly these hints to validate real

509
00:32:32,538 --> 00:32:33,729
data at runtime.

510
00:32:33,949 --> 00:32:36,115
That's where hints become really powerful.

511
00:32:37,415 --> 00:32:42,830
HTTP is the language of web
APIs, and it is simpler than it sounds.

512
00:32:43,180 --> 00:32:48,901
There are two sides: a client, which is
your Python code, and a server, such as a

513
00:32:48,901 --> 00:32:50,384
model provider's API.

514
00:32:50,734 --> 00:32:52,351
The client sends a request.

515
00:32:52,571 --> 00:32:58,859
It has a method, such as GET to read or
POST to send data, a URL, headers with extra

516
00:32:58,859 --> 00:33:02,752
information like your API
key, and sometimes a body.

517
00:33:03,102 --> 00:33:09,351
The server sends back a response: a
status code saying how it went, headers, and a

518
00:33:09,351 --> 00:33:12,857
body, which for AI
APIs is almost always JSON.

519
00:33:14,157 --> 00:33:16,470
Codes in the two hundreds mean success.

520
00:33:16,690 --> 00:33:19,873
Two hundred is OK, two oh one is created.

521
00:33:20,223 --> 00:33:23,191
Four hundreds mean the
problem is on your side.

522
00:33:23,411 --> 00:33:29,069
Bad input, a missing or wrong
API key, or a URL that doesn't exist.

523
00:33:29,289 --> 00:33:30,609
Retrying won't help.

524
00:33:30,829 --> 00:33:32,008
Fix the request.

525
00:33:32,358 --> 00:33:34,896
Four twenty-nine deserves its own card.

526
00:33:35,116 --> 00:33:36,846
It means you hit a rate limit.

527
00:33:37,066 --> 00:33:42,556
Every model API has them, and Module
2 shows how to wait and retry properly.

528
00:33:42,906 --> 00:33:45,673
And five hundreds mean
the server had a problem.

529
00:33:45,893 --> 00:33:49,764
Those are often temporary,
so a careful retry can succeed.

530
00:33:51,064 --> 00:33:52,919
Now let's make real requests.

531
00:33:53,139 --> 00:33:59,210
So that this works offline, I'm serving
the project's sample data from a small local

532
00:33:59,210 --> 00:34:02,969
web server, using
Python's built-in http dot server.

533
00:34:03,319 --> 00:34:09,065
httpx dot get sends a GET
request and returns a response object.

534
00:34:09,285 --> 00:34:10,725
Always pass a timeout.

535
00:34:10,945 --> 00:34:14,528
A network call that hangs
forever will freeze your agent.

536
00:34:14,748 --> 00:34:18,545
The response tells us the status
code and the content type header.

537
00:34:18,895 --> 00:34:24,408
Raise for status turns any error status
into an exception, so failures can't slip by

538
00:34:24,408 --> 00:34:25,064
unnoticed.

539
00:34:25,284 --> 00:34:29,287
Then dot json parses the
body into Python objects.

540
00:34:29,637 --> 00:34:35,388
And here we request a file that doesn't
exist, and catch the HTTP status error to

541
00:34:35,388 --> 00:34:36,878
read its status code.

542
00:34:38,178 --> 00:34:40,851
First, start the local
server in the background.

543
00:34:41,071 --> 00:34:43,431
It serves the files in the data folder.

544
00:34:43,781 --> 00:34:45,067
Then run the script.

545
00:34:45,287 --> 00:34:50,815
Status two hundred with a JSON content
type, ten records loaded, and a clean four oh

546
00:34:50,815 --> 00:34:52,526
four for the missing file.

547
00:34:53,826 --> 00:34:59,028
A language model call can take several
seconds, and during that time your program is

548
00:34:59,028 --> 00:35:00,825
just waiting for the network.

549
00:35:01,175 --> 00:35:06,582
If an agent needs three independent
answers and asks for them one by one, the waits

550
00:35:06,582 --> 00:35:07,038
add up.

551
00:35:07,258 --> 00:35:10,438
Three calls of one
second each take three seconds.

552
00:35:10,788 --> 00:35:15,741
With async, you start all three, wait
for them together, and finish in about one

553
00:35:15,741 --> 00:35:16,174
second.

554
00:35:16,394 --> 00:35:18,235
That's not more computing power.

555
00:35:18,455 --> 00:35:20,609
It's simply not wasting time waiting.

556
00:35:21,909 --> 00:35:25,984
Async def defines a
coroutine, a function that can pause.

557
00:35:26,204 --> 00:35:31,864
Await means: pause here until this
finishes, and let other work run meanwhile.

558
00:35:31,864 --> 00:35:36,145
asyncio dot sleep stands in
for waiting on a network reply.

559
00:35:36,495 --> 00:35:41,257
This version awaits each call in a
loop, so they still run one after another.

560
00:35:41,607 --> 00:35:47,220
This version passes all three to
asyncio dot gather, which runs them concurrently

561
00:35:47,220 --> 00:35:51,239
and returns the results in
the same order you passed them.

562
00:35:51,589 --> 00:35:56,955
Finally, asyncio dot run starts
the event loop that drives coroutines.

563
00:35:57,175 --> 00:35:58,787
We time both versions.

564
00:36:00,087 --> 00:36:01,340
A real measurement.

565
00:36:01,560 --> 00:36:05,001
Three seconds one by
one, one second concurrently.

566
00:36:06,301 --> 00:36:09,072
httpx has an async client too.

567
00:36:09,292 --> 00:36:14,473
The fetch coroutine awaits the request
and returns how many records came back.

568
00:36:14,823 --> 00:36:19,475
Async with opens one shared
client, and closes it properly afterwards.

569
00:36:19,695 --> 00:36:22,878
Gather fetches every URL at the same time.

570
00:36:23,098 --> 00:36:26,186
This is exactly how the
project's fetch command works.

571
00:36:27,486 --> 00:36:31,973
Both files downloaded
concurrently: ten records and five records.

572
00:36:33,273 --> 00:36:36,008
Await only works inside an async function.

573
00:36:36,358 --> 00:36:41,301
Start the event loop once, at the top
of your program, with asyncio dot run.

574
00:36:41,651 --> 00:36:44,890
Async speeds up waiting,
for the network or the disk.

575
00:36:45,110 --> 00:36:47,519
It doesn't make heavy calculations faster.

576
00:36:47,869 --> 00:36:52,541
And never call blocking code, like
time dot sleep, inside a coroutine.

577
00:36:52,761 --> 00:36:54,546
It freezes every other task.

578
00:36:54,766 --> 00:36:56,844
Use the async version instead.

579
00:36:58,144 --> 00:36:58,951
Quick check.

580
00:36:59,171 --> 00:37:02,094
An API replies with
status four twenty-nine.

581
00:37:02,314 --> 00:37:04,671
What does it mean, and
what should your code do?

582
00:37:10,371 --> 00:37:11,503
Too many requests.

583
00:37:11,723 --> 00:37:14,413
You hit a rate limit, so wait, then retry.

584
00:37:14,633 --> 00:37:18,074
You'll implement this
properly, with backoff, in Module 2.

585
00:37:19,374 --> 00:37:24,646
Type hints document your intent, and a
type checker verifies them before the code

586
00:37:24,646 --> 00:37:24,971
runs.

587
00:37:25,321 --> 00:37:31,221
HTTP is a request and a response, and
the status code tells you how it went.

588
00:37:31,571 --> 00:37:35,640
Use httpx with a
timeout, and raise for status.

589
00:37:35,990 --> 00:37:40,462
And use async, await, and
gather to wait for many calls at once.

590
00:37:40,812 --> 00:37:43,852
You now have every
Python skill the project needs.

591
00:37:44,072 --> 00:37:47,914
In the next lesson, we start
building the Python AI Toolkit.

592
00:37:49,222 --> 00:37:50,185
Time to build.

593
00:37:50,405 --> 00:37:55,878
Over the next two lessons, you'll write
the Python AI Toolkit from an empty folder

594
00:37:55,878 --> 00:37:57,881
to a tested command line tool.

595
00:37:58,101 --> 00:38:01,188
Every concept from this
module appears somewhere in it.

596
00:38:01,408 --> 00:38:05,129
In this lesson we plan it,
model the data, and validate it.

597
00:38:06,429 --> 00:38:12,200
Every AI team needs to answer simple
questions about their language model usage.

598
00:38:12,420 --> 00:38:13,882
How many requests failed?

599
00:38:14,102 --> 00:38:15,980
How many tokens did we use?

600
00:38:16,200 --> 00:38:17,660
Roughly what did it cost?

601
00:38:17,880 --> 00:38:19,691
Here is what our tool must do.

602
00:38:20,041 --> 00:38:24,973
Read a JSON log of model API
calls, with one record per request.

603
00:38:25,323 --> 00:38:31,116
Validate every record, and explain each
problem precisely, without crashing on bad

604
00:38:31,116 --> 00:38:31,469
data.

605
00:38:31,819 --> 00:38:37,645
Summarise the good records: success
rate, tokens, estimated cost, latency, and the

606
00:38:37,645 --> 00:38:38,853
most common tags.

607
00:38:39,203 --> 00:38:43,891
Write that report as Markdown for
people, or JSON for other programs.

608
00:38:44,241 --> 00:38:48,057
Download logs from
several URLs at the same time.

609
00:38:48,407 --> 00:38:53,273
And take its settings from environment
variables, with automated tests for all of

610
00:38:53,273 --> 00:38:53,453
it.

611
00:38:54,753 --> 00:38:57,468
Before writing code,
decide how to split it.

612
00:38:57,688 --> 00:38:59,433
Each file gets one job.

613
00:38:59,783 --> 00:39:04,732
At the top, cli dot py reads the
command line and calls everything else.

614
00:39:04,952 --> 00:39:08,479
It's the only part that
knows about arguments and printing.

615
00:39:08,829 --> 00:39:14,445
The loader reads a file, and models turns
each raw dictionary into a validated usage

616
00:39:14,445 --> 00:39:14,913
record.

617
00:39:15,263 --> 00:39:18,482
Stats calculates the
summary, and report formats it.

618
00:39:18,702 --> 00:39:22,833
Calculating and formatting are
separate, so each is easy to test.

619
00:39:23,183 --> 00:39:28,681
Config reads settings and prices,
and fetch downloads logs over HTTP.

620
00:39:29,031 --> 00:39:31,690
And errors defines our exception classes.

621
00:39:31,910 --> 00:39:37,461
Any module can raise a toolkit error, and
the command line catches it in exactly one

622
00:39:37,461 --> 00:39:37,857
place.

623
00:39:39,157 --> 00:39:40,264
Here's the input.

624
00:39:40,484 --> 00:39:43,647
A JSON list, with one
object per model call.

625
00:39:43,997 --> 00:39:49,358
Each record has an id, the model
used, and the prompt and completion tokens.

626
00:39:49,578 --> 00:39:55,018
Prompt tokens are what you sent, and
completion tokens are what the model generated.

627
00:39:55,238 --> 00:39:57,765
Providers usually price them differently.

628
00:39:58,115 --> 00:40:05,535
It also has the latency in
milliseconds, a status of ok or error, a list of tags

629
00:40:05,535 --> 00:40:10,730
describing the task, and a
timestamp in ISO 8601 format.

630
00:40:10,950 --> 00:40:16,204
Elsewhere in this file, request four
failed after thirty seconds, which looks like a

631
00:40:16,204 --> 00:40:16,704
timeout.

632
00:40:16,924 --> 00:40:19,778
Real logs contain
exactly this kind of mess.

633
00:40:21,078 --> 00:40:25,031
We start with the errors,
because every other module uses them.

634
00:40:25,251 --> 00:40:29,571
Toolkit error is the base class
for anything we raise on purpose.

635
00:40:29,921 --> 00:40:32,804
Validation error is a more specific kind.

636
00:40:33,024 --> 00:40:38,052
It calls the parent's init with a
readable message, and also stores which field

637
00:40:38,052 --> 00:40:39,961
failed, so tests can check it.

638
00:40:41,261 --> 00:40:42,582
Now the data model.

639
00:40:42,802 --> 00:40:47,476
Allowed status is a set, because we
only ever ask, is this status in the set?

640
00:40:47,826 --> 00:40:49,484
Require is a small helper.

641
00:40:49,704 --> 00:40:55,108
It returns a field's value, or raises
a validation error saying the field is

642
00:40:55,108 --> 00:40:58,165
required, instead of
a confusing key error.

643
00:40:58,515 --> 00:41:01,230
This helper checks for
a non-negative integer.

644
00:41:01,450 --> 00:41:03,022
There's one subtle trap here.

645
00:41:03,242 --> 00:41:08,371
In Python, True and False are a kind
of int, so we reject bools explicitly.

646
00:41:08,721 --> 00:41:13,113
The record itself is a
dataclass, with a type hint for every field.

647
00:41:13,333 --> 00:41:19,108
Frozen equals True makes it read-only,
so nothing can quietly modify a record after

648
00:41:19,108 --> 00:41:19,873
validation.

649
00:41:20,223 --> 00:41:22,107
Total tokens is a property.

650
00:41:22,327 --> 00:41:27,450
It's calculated from the other fields,
but you use it like a normal attribute,

651
00:41:27,450 --> 00:41:28,764
without parentheses.

652
00:41:30,064 --> 00:41:33,762
From dict is a class
method, an alternative constructor.

653
00:41:33,982 --> 00:41:39,394
It receives the class itself as cls,
and builds a record from a raw dictionary.

654
00:41:39,614 --> 00:41:42,775
First, it checks that the
item is a dictionary at all.

655
00:41:43,125 --> 00:41:44,705
Then each field gets checked.

656
00:41:44,925 --> 00:41:48,140
The id and the model
must be non-empty strings.

657
00:41:48,490 --> 00:41:54,341
Latency must be a non-negative number,
either an int or a float, but again, not a

658
00:41:54,341 --> 00:41:54,702
bool.

659
00:41:55,052 --> 00:41:57,013
The status must be in the allowed set.

660
00:41:57,233 --> 00:42:01,623
The message lists the valid
options, which makes the error easy to fix.

661
00:42:01,973 --> 00:42:07,411
Tags are optional, so we use get with an
empty list as the default, and then check

662
00:42:07,411 --> 00:42:09,267
that it's a list of strings.

663
00:42:09,617 --> 00:42:13,957
For the timestamp, datetime
dot fromisoformat does the parsing.

664
00:42:14,177 --> 00:42:19,565
If it fails, we raise our own error,
from None, so the user sees one clear message

665
00:42:19,565 --> 00:42:21,799
instead of two chained tracebacks.

666
00:42:22,149 --> 00:42:25,886
Finally, we call cls
with every validated value.

667
00:42:26,106 --> 00:42:29,342
If we get here, the
record is guaranteed to be valid.

668
00:42:30,642 --> 00:42:35,877
Let's try the model in the interactive
Python prompt, before any other code exists.

669
00:42:36,097 --> 00:42:39,042
Start it by typing
python in the project folder.

670
00:42:39,392 --> 00:42:41,946
We build a valid dictionary step by step.

671
00:42:42,166 --> 00:42:45,942
From dict gives us a
record, and total tokens is fifteen.

672
00:42:46,292 --> 00:42:48,619
Now change one field to minus one.

673
00:42:48,839 --> 00:42:52,614
Our validation error names
the field and the bad value.

674
00:42:52,834 --> 00:42:58,077
Double star row copies the dictionary,
and the new key overrides the old value.

675
00:42:59,377 --> 00:43:02,694
Read JSON handles the
two ways reading can fail.

676
00:43:02,914 --> 00:43:06,782
The file might not exist,
or it might not be valid JSON.

677
00:43:07,002 --> 00:43:12,464
Both become a toolkit error with a clear
message, including the line and column for

678
00:43:12,464 --> 00:43:13,056
bad JSON.

679
00:43:13,406 --> 00:43:18,920
Load records returns a tuple of two
lists: the valid records, and a message for

680
00:43:18,920 --> 00:43:20,176
every invalid one.

681
00:43:20,396 --> 00:43:22,786
The top level of the file must be a list.

682
00:43:23,136 --> 00:43:27,949
Then we loop with enumerate, so we
can report record numbers starting at one.

683
00:43:28,169 --> 00:43:33,761
Each record that fails validation is
recorded as a problem, and the loop carries on.

684
00:43:33,981 --> 00:43:37,429
One bad record never stops
the good ones from loading.

685
00:43:38,729 --> 00:43:44,046
Settings is a small frozen dataclass
with the two things we can configure: the

686
00:43:44,046 --> 00:43:46,500
prices file, and the currency label.

687
00:43:46,850 --> 00:43:49,710
Load dot env is our tiny dot env reader.

688
00:43:49,930 --> 00:43:55,516
It skips blank lines and comments,
splits each line at the first equals sign, and

689
00:43:55,516 --> 00:44:01,102
uses set default, so a variable that's
already set in the real environment always

690
00:44:01,102 --> 00:44:02,412
wins over the file.

691
00:44:02,762 --> 00:44:06,615
Load settings reads both
variables, with sensible defaults.

692
00:44:06,965 --> 00:44:12,488
And load prices reads the prices file,
skipping keys that start with an underscore,

693
00:44:12,488 --> 00:44:14,218
which we use for comments.

694
00:44:14,438 --> 00:44:18,969
These are example prices for
fictional models, per million tokens.

695
00:44:19,189 --> 00:44:21,423
They are not real provider prices.

696
00:44:22,723 --> 00:44:26,683
Prices is a type alias, a
short name for a long type hint.

697
00:44:27,033 --> 00:44:31,720
Model stats holds running totals
for one model, all starting at zero.

698
00:44:32,070 --> 00:44:34,408
Summary holds every number in the report.

699
00:44:34,628 --> 00:44:39,820
Notice the default factories again,
and two properties that calculate the total

700
00:44:39,820 --> 00:44:43,961
tokens and the success rate,
guarding against dividing by zero.

701
00:44:44,311 --> 00:44:50,148
Estimate cost multiplies prompt and
completion tokens by their prices, and divides

702
00:44:50,148 --> 00:44:51,073
by a million.

703
00:44:51,293 --> 00:44:54,382
An unknown model returns
None instead of crashing.

704
00:44:55,682 --> 00:45:01,403
Summarize prepares a dictionary of
per-model stats, a counter for tags, a set of

705
00:45:01,403 --> 00:45:04,335
models with no price, and a running cost.

706
00:45:04,685 --> 00:45:06,474
One loop does all the counting.

707
00:45:06,694 --> 00:45:10,426
Set default creates a model's
stats the first time we see it.

708
00:45:10,646 --> 00:45:15,823
Unpriced models are collected, not
fatal, and the counter tallies every tag.

709
00:45:16,173 --> 00:45:21,587
Finally, comprehensions and sum compute
the totals, and most common gives the top

710
00:45:21,587 --> 00:45:22,322
three tags.

711
00:45:22,542 --> 00:45:25,099
Everything from
lesson two, working together.

712
00:45:26,399 --> 00:45:30,354
The command line doesn't exist
yet, but the pieces already work.

713
00:45:30,574 --> 00:45:32,649
Let's call them from the Python prompt.

714
00:45:32,999 --> 00:45:38,097
Loading the broken file gives one
valid record and four problems, each with a

715
00:45:38,097 --> 00:45:39,156
precise message.

716
00:45:39,506 --> 00:45:44,946
And summarising the clean sample
gives ten requests, eight successful, eleven

717
00:45:44,946 --> 00:45:50,315
thousand and ninety-five tokens, the
estimated cost, and the top three tags.

718
00:45:51,615 --> 00:45:54,374
We planned small
modules with one job each.

719
00:45:54,724 --> 00:46:00,296
We validate data at the edge, turning raw
dictionaries into trusted records or clear

720
00:46:00,296 --> 00:46:00,760
errors.

721
00:46:01,110 --> 00:46:04,346
We collect problems instead
of crashing on the first one.

722
00:46:04,696 --> 00:46:07,188
And we keep calculating
separate from formatting.

723
00:46:07,538 --> 00:46:12,755
In part two, we write the report, the
downloader, and the command line, then run the

724
00:46:12,755 --> 00:46:15,674
finished tool, test
it, and wrap up the module.

725
00:46:16,998 --> 00:46:18,023
Welcome back.

726
00:46:18,243 --> 00:46:19,688
The data layer works.

727
00:46:19,908 --> 00:46:25,007
Now we add the report writer, the
downloader, and the command line, then run the

728
00:46:25,007 --> 00:46:30,361
finished tool, test it, fix the common
errors, and finish the module with a quiz and

729
00:46:30,361 --> 00:46:31,126
a challenge.

730
00:46:32,426 --> 00:46:35,690
Render markdown takes a
summary and returns text.

731
00:46:35,910 --> 00:46:40,897
It doesn't print anything, so it's easy
to test, and the caller decides where the

732
00:46:40,897 --> 00:46:41,512
text goes.

733
00:46:41,862 --> 00:46:47,169
The report is built as a list of
lines, using f-string formats from lesson two.

734
00:46:47,389 --> 00:46:52,861
Colon point one percent shows a
percentage, and colon point four f shows four

735
00:46:52,861 --> 00:46:53,927
decimal places.

736
00:46:54,277 --> 00:46:57,033
A Markdown table has one row per model.

737
00:46:57,383 --> 00:47:03,207
Then the top tags, numbered with
enumerate, a warning if any model had no price, and

738
00:47:03,207 --> 00:47:06,396
finally join glues
everything into one string.

739
00:47:07,696 --> 00:47:13,112
The JSON report builds nested
dictionaries in exactly the shape we want, rounding

740
00:47:13,112 --> 00:47:15,520
the floats so the output stays tidy.

741
00:47:15,870 --> 00:47:21,052
A dictionary comprehension builds the
per-model section, and a list comprehension

742
00:47:21,052 --> 00:47:22,076
builds the tags.

743
00:47:22,296 --> 00:47:27,058
Sets aren't valid JSON, so the
unpriced models become a sorted list.

744
00:47:27,408 --> 00:47:30,280
And json dot dumps turns it all into text.

745
00:47:31,580 --> 00:47:34,758
Fetch one is the async
request from lesson five.

746
00:47:34,978 --> 00:47:38,660
Get the URL, raise for
status, and parse the JSON.

747
00:47:39,010 --> 00:47:42,686
Each kind of failure
becomes one readable toolkit error.

748
00:47:42,906 --> 00:47:48,760
An error status, a network problem like
a refused connection, or a body that isn't

749
00:47:48,760 --> 00:47:49,117
JSON.

750
00:47:49,467 --> 00:47:51,749
And the body has to be a list of records.

751
00:47:52,099 --> 00:47:58,213
Fetch all opens one client, gathers
every download at the same time, and flattens

752
00:47:58,213 --> 00:48:02,516
the batches into one list,
keeping the order of the URLs.

753
00:48:02,736 --> 00:48:05,640
The optional transport
parameter is for testing.

754
00:48:05,860 --> 00:48:09,113
It lets tests replace the
real network with a fake one.

755
00:48:10,413 --> 00:48:15,388
The standard library's argparse
turns command line text into Python values.

756
00:48:15,608 --> 00:48:19,312
It also generates help text
and error messages for free.

757
00:48:19,662 --> 00:48:23,917
Sub parsers give us separate
commands, like git commit and git push.

758
00:48:24,137 --> 00:48:29,289
Validate takes one file, and type
equals Path converts it to a Path object.

759
00:48:29,639 --> 00:48:35,306
Report adds options: a format
restricted to two choices, an optional output file,

760
00:48:35,306 --> 00:48:38,385
and a strict flag
that is True when present.

761
00:48:38,735 --> 00:48:43,340
Fetch accepts one or more
URLs, and requires an output file.

762
00:48:44,640 --> 00:48:48,269
Each command is a
function that returns an exit code.

763
00:48:48,489 --> 00:48:54,157
Validate prints every problem and
returns one if anything was wrong, so scripts and

764
00:48:54,157 --> 00:48:56,343
CI pipelines can detect failure.

765
00:48:56,693 --> 00:48:58,818
Report loads settings and records.

766
00:48:59,038 --> 00:49:04,272
Warnings go to standard error, so they
never mix into a report that's being saved or

767
00:49:04,272 --> 00:49:04,645
piped.

768
00:49:04,865 --> 00:49:07,733
With strict, any
invalid record is a failure.

769
00:49:07,953 --> 00:49:11,897
Then we summarise and choose
a renderer, Markdown or JSON.

770
00:49:12,247 --> 00:49:15,951
The text is written to a
file if out was given, or printed.

771
00:49:16,301 --> 00:49:21,795
Fetch uses asyncio dot run to call
our async function from normal code.

772
00:49:22,145 --> 00:49:27,192
A dictionary maps each command name to
its function, so there's no long chain of

773
00:49:27,192 --> 00:49:27,444
ifs.

774
00:49:27,664 --> 00:49:32,953
And main is the single place that
catches toolkit errors, printing one clean line

775
00:49:32,953 --> 00:49:35,695
and returning one, instead of a traceback.

776
00:49:36,995 --> 00:49:41,128
And dunder main is what runs
when you type python dash m toolkit.

777
00:49:41,348 --> 00:49:46,878
It calls main, and raises system exit
with the return value, which becomes the

778
00:49:46,878 --> 00:49:48,296
program's exit code.

779
00:49:49,596 --> 00:49:51,027
Let's run the finished tool.

780
00:49:51,247 --> 00:49:55,295
Help is generated by
argparse, listing the three commands.

781
00:49:55,645 --> 00:49:57,641
The sample file is fully valid.

782
00:49:57,991 --> 00:50:01,249
The broken file
reports four precise problems.

783
00:50:01,599 --> 00:50:06,489
And the exit code is one, which is
how other programs know validation failed.

784
00:50:07,789 --> 00:50:09,141
The Markdown report.

785
00:50:09,361 --> 00:50:12,042
Ten requests with an
eighty percent success rate.

786
00:50:12,262 --> 00:50:15,498
And notice the average
latency, over four seconds.

787
00:50:15,718 --> 00:50:18,741
That's because one failed
request took thirty seconds.

788
00:50:18,961 --> 00:50:24,302
An average hides outliers like that,
which is why one of this module's challenges

789
00:50:24,302 --> 00:50:26,214
asks you to add a percentile.

790
00:50:27,514 --> 00:50:33,299
With format json and out, the report is
saved to a file instead, and the folder is

791
00:50:33,299 --> 00:50:34,851
created automatically.

792
00:50:35,201 --> 00:50:40,043
Here's the start of that JSON file,
ready for a dashboard or another program.

793
00:50:40,393 --> 00:50:45,078
In strict mode, the broken file
fails, with the warning on standard error.

794
00:50:45,428 --> 00:50:47,397
And settings come from the environment.

795
00:50:47,617 --> 00:50:52,857
Point the prices file at something that
doesn't exist, and you get one clear error

796
00:50:52,857 --> 00:50:54,071
line, no traceback.

797
00:50:55,371 --> 00:50:56,475
Finally, fetch.

798
00:50:56,695 --> 00:51:02,450
A local web server is serving the data
folder on port eighty-seven sixty-five, just

799
00:51:02,450 --> 00:51:03,837
like in lesson five.

800
00:51:04,187 --> 00:51:08,650
To keep the command short, we store
the base address in a shell variable.

801
00:51:09,000 --> 00:51:14,418
Both files download concurrently, and
fifteen records are merged into one file.

802
00:51:14,768 --> 00:51:20,656
Validating the merged file finds the
eleven good records and the same four problems.

803
00:51:21,006 --> 00:51:25,995
And if a server can't be reached,
again, one clear line explains what happened.

804
00:51:27,295 --> 00:51:29,556
A demo shows that the tool works today.

805
00:51:29,776 --> 00:51:32,220
Tests make sure it keeps
working when you change it.

806
00:51:32,440 --> 00:51:33,661
We use pytest.

807
00:51:34,011 --> 00:51:39,339
A pytest test is just a function whose
name starts with test, using plain assert

808
00:51:39,339 --> 00:51:40,071
statements.

809
00:51:40,291 --> 00:51:41,736
Good row is a fixture.

810
00:51:41,956 --> 00:51:47,986
Pytest sees the parameter name and
passes in a fresh valid dictionary, defined once

811
00:51:47,986 --> 00:51:49,367
in conftest dot py.

812
00:51:49,717 --> 00:51:52,796
Parametrize runs the
same test once per value.

813
00:51:53,016 --> 00:51:58,302
Here, five tests, each deleting a
different required field, and checking that the

814
00:51:58,302 --> 00:51:59,802
error names that field.

815
00:52:00,152 --> 00:52:05,515
This one feeds in nine kinds of bad
data, including the sneaky True posing as a

816
00:52:05,515 --> 00:52:09,317
number, and checks each is
rejected for the right field.

817
00:52:10,617 --> 00:52:17,032
Tests must not depend on a real network,
which could be slow or down. httpx's mock

818
00:52:17,032 --> 00:52:22,900
transport replaces the network with
a function that returns fake responses.

819
00:52:23,250 --> 00:52:26,794
So we can check that two
downloads are merged in the right order.

820
00:52:27,144 --> 00:52:32,316
And that a four oh four, or a response
that isn't a list, becomes a toolkit error.

821
00:52:32,536 --> 00:52:35,640
These run in
milliseconds, with no server at all.

822
00:52:36,940 --> 00:52:38,149
Run the whole suite.

823
00:52:38,369 --> 00:52:41,748
Forty-two tests, all
passing, in a fraction of a second.

824
00:52:42,098 --> 00:52:45,397
With dash v, pytest
lists each test by name.

825
00:52:45,747 --> 00:52:49,193
And mypy confirms that all
our type hints are consistent.

826
00:52:50,493 --> 00:52:53,529
First, running a file
inside the package directly.

827
00:52:53,749 --> 00:52:58,661
Python can't find the toolkit package,
because it searches from the toolkit folder

828
00:52:58,661 --> 00:52:59,080
itself.

829
00:52:59,300 --> 00:53:03,603
The fix: stay in the project
root and run python dash m toolkit.

830
00:53:03,953 --> 00:53:06,441
Second, forgetting
the virtual environment.

831
00:53:06,791 --> 00:53:12,771
With the environment deactivated, the
system Python doesn't have httpx installed.

832
00:53:12,991 --> 00:53:15,602
The fix is to activate dot venv again.

833
00:53:16,902 --> 00:53:22,531
Is not valid JSON usually means a
trailing comma, single quotes, or a comment.

834
00:53:22,751 --> 00:53:25,048
The message gives you the line and column.

835
00:53:25,398 --> 00:53:29,854
File not found usually means you
ran the command from another folder.

836
00:53:30,074 --> 00:53:32,302
Relative paths start
from where you run it.

837
00:53:32,652 --> 00:53:38,446
On Windows, if PowerShell refuses to
run the activate script, run Set Execution

838
00:53:38,446 --> 00:53:42,333
Policy for the current
user with Remote Signed, once.

839
00:53:42,683 --> 00:53:48,194
And if text comes out garbled, make
sure every read and write uses utf eight.

840
00:53:49,494 --> 00:53:54,879
You used lists, dictionaries, and sets,
and built conversations as lists of message

841
00:53:54,879 --> 00:53:55,722
dictionaries.

842
00:53:56,072 --> 00:53:59,581
You wrote functions,
dataclasses, and your own exceptions.

843
00:53:59,931 --> 00:54:05,124
You organised a package, and worked
with files, JSON, and environment variables.

844
00:54:05,474 --> 00:54:11,090
You added type hints, made HTTP
requests, and ran downloads concurrently.

845
00:54:11,440 --> 00:54:16,571
And you built a tested command line
tool with clear errors and proper exit codes.

846
00:54:17,871 --> 00:54:19,700
Module quiz, question one.

847
00:54:19,920 --> 00:54:22,189
An agent may only use two tools.

848
00:54:22,409 --> 00:54:26,082
Which structure gives the fastest
check for whether a tool is allowed?

849
00:54:31,782 --> 00:54:32,462
A set.

850
00:54:32,682 --> 00:54:36,522
Membership checks on a set
stay fast however large it grows.

851
00:54:37,822 --> 00:54:38,858
Question two.

852
00:54:39,078 --> 00:54:42,291
What does frozen equals
True add to our usage record?

853
00:54:47,991 --> 00:54:51,344
Fields can't be changed
after the record is created.

854
00:54:51,564 --> 00:54:55,337
And note, a dataclass does
not validate types by itself.

855
00:54:55,557 --> 00:54:57,806
That's why from dict checks every field.

856
00:54:59,106 --> 00:55:00,168
Question three.

857
00:55:00,388 --> 00:55:04,163
Which block runs every time,
whether or not an error happens?

858
00:55:09,863 --> 00:55:10,635
Finally.

859
00:55:10,855 --> 00:55:12,644
That's why it's used for cleanup.

860
00:55:13,944 --> 00:55:15,006
Question four.

861
00:55:15,226 --> 00:55:18,195
Why does fetch all use asyncio dot gather?

862
00:55:23,895 --> 00:55:29,042
To download everything concurrently,
so the total time is roughly the slowest

863
00:55:29,042 --> 00:55:31,516
download, not the sum of all of them.

864
00:55:32,816 --> 00:55:34,078
And question five.

865
00:55:34,298 --> 00:55:37,297
Where should the real API
key for your project live?

866
00:55:42,997 --> 00:55:47,361
In an environment variable, or
a dot env file that git ignores.

867
00:55:47,581 --> 00:55:51,173
The example file only
ever holds fake placeholders.

868
